Figure 11 compares the residual inflammation at injection sites two weeks post-injection with either aluminum hydroxide (AH) or Nano-11 adjuvant. The left panel shows significant granulomatous inflammation, characterized by extensive aggregates of macrophages and scattered eosinophils, in muscle injected with AH. In contrast, the right panel, representing the Nano-11 injection site, exhibits considerably less inflammation, with relatively few macrophages forming slender cords between muscle fibers. This visual evidence supports the finding that Nano-11 induces a more transient and less severe inflammatory response than aluminum adjuvants, highlighting its favorable safety profile for vaccine applications.